<header class="sk-header site-header"><div class="sk-header__inner"><a aria-label="Robert DeVore home" class="site-brand" href="/"><span aria-hidden="true">RD://</span> Robert DeVore</a><nav aria-label="Primary" class="site-nav site-nav--desktop"><a href="/blog/">Writing</a><a href="/projects/">Projects</a><a href="/courses/">Courses</a><a href="/about/">About</a><a href="/contact/">Contact</a></nav><details class="site-menu"><summary>Menu</summary><nav aria-label="Mobile"><a href="/blog/">Writing</a><a href="/projects/">Projects</a><a href="/courses/">Courses</a><a href="/about/">About</a><a href="/contact/">Contact</a></nav></details></div></header>
<main class="about-page about-profile" id="main-content">
<header class="signal-hero signal-hero--page signal-art--b about-hero"><picture class="signal-hero__field"><source media="(max-width: 48rem)" srcset="/assets/art/signal-b-720.webp?v=1.0.9"/><img alt="" height="800" loading="eager" src="/assets/art/signal-b-1920.webp?v=1.0.9" width="1920"/></picture><div class="signal-hero__inner"><p class="signal-kicker">About / Robert DeVore</p><h1 class="signal-title">Software engineer.<br/>Security researcher.<br/>Builder.</h1><p class="signal-lede">More than 20 years building commercial products, open-source software, and developer tools.</p><div class="signal-actions"><a class="sk-button" href="#built">See what I’ve built</a><a class="sk-button" data-variant="secondary" href="#security">Explore the research</a></div></div></header>
<nav aria-label="On this page" class="about-nav sk-container"><a href="#background">Background</a><a href="#security">Security research</a><a href="#built">Software</a><a href="#writing">Writing &amp; education</a><a href="#approach">Approach</a></nav>
<section aria-labelledby="background-title" class="about-section sk-container about-editorial" id="background"><div><p class="section-index">01 / Background</p><h2 id="background-title">From the web<br/>to languages and tools.</h2></div><div class="about-prose"><p class="about-intro">I’m Robert DeVore, a software engineer, security researcher, open-source developer, and technical writer. I’ve spent more than 20 years building commercial products, publishing systems, and tools that help people do their work.</p><p>I started with PHP, WordPress, and publishing systems. Building plugins led to commercial products, along with the maintenance, support, and integrations that customers relied on.</p><p>I’ve worked independently and with agencies on custom software, security audits, performance, and ongoing support. That work shaped how I approach architecture and technical leadership: a system has to make sense to the people who run it, change it, and inherit it.</p><p>Today I build programming languages, developer tools, and systems for AI-assisted engineering. Kujo brings that work together. I want people to understand how their software works, verify its behavior, and maintain it long after launch.</p><p class="about-source-note">From the archive: <a href="/im-freelancing-again/">agency work and independent development</a> · <a href="/the-past-present-and-future-of-wp-dispensary/">building a long-lived product</a>.</p></div>
<ol class="about-progression"><li><h3>Foundation</h3><p class="about-progression-topic">The open web</p><p>PHP, WordPress, publishing workflows, and software built around real users.</p></li><li><h3>Ownership</h3><p class="about-progression-topic">Products &amp; teams</p><p>Commercial plugins, client systems, maintenance, and the decisions that outlast a release.</p></li><li><h3>Investigation</h3><p class="about-progression-topic">Security research</p><p>Tracing trust boundaries, reporting vulnerabilities responsibly, and learning from failure.</p></li><li><h3>Current work</h3><p class="about-progression-topic">Languages &amp; tools</p><p>Kujo, local applications, and inspectable infrastructure for AI-assisted engineering.</p></li></ol></section>
<section aria-labelledby="security-title" class="about-security" id="security"><div class="sk-container"><div class="about-editorial"><div><p class="section-index">02 / Security research</p><h2 id="security-title">I don’t just build software.<br/>I find where it breaks.</h2></div><div class="about-prose"><p>My research examines how software handles untrusted input, enforces permissions, and fails when those checks are missing.</p><p>My published disclosures cover WordPress plugins, from cross-site scripting to authorization failures. I report findings through coordinated disclosure programs so maintainers can address them and users have a public record to consult.</p><div class="about-research-total" data-no-scramble=""><strong>40</strong><span>credited discoveries on Wordfence<br/><small>Individually cross-checked against CVE Program records</small></span></div><p class="about-source-note">Verified 21 October 2026: 40 discoveries map to 40 unique published CVE IDs. Patchstack’s 31 reports overlap this set; they are not an additional 31 discoveries.</p><p><a href="https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/vulnerabilities/researchers/robert-devore">Wordfence researcher profile</a> · <a href="https://patchstack.com/database/researchers/770fb791-e511-4ce8-8c93-c3f912d443df">Patchstack researcher profile</a></p></div></div>
<div class="about-selected" data-no-scramble=""><h3>Selected disclosures</h3><p>Six examples from the verified disclosure record.</p><div class="about-selected-grid" data-no-scramble=""><article><p class="about-record-id">CVE-2025-5537 <span>Medium · 6.4</span></p><h4>Lightbox &amp; Modal Popup WordPress Plugin – FooBox</h4><p>Stored cross-site scripting</p><a href="https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/vulnerabilities/id/b5fd2163-b8ef-4dd1-a12b-cd9187145134?source=cve">Read advisory <span class="sk-visually-hidden">for CVE-2025-5537</span>↗</a></article><article><p class="about-record-id">CVE-2025-5292 <span>Medium · 6.4</span></p><h4>Element Pack – Widgets, Templates &amp; Addons for Elementor</h4><p>DOM-based stored cross-site scripting</p><a href="https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/vulnerabilities/id/ab8dfdd8-820c-4066-8014-2cb5b9f935a4?source=cve">Read advisory <span class="sk-visually-hidden">for CVE-2025-5292</span>↗</a></article><article><p class="about-record-id">CVE-2025-32259 <span>Medium · 5.3</span></p><h4>WP ULike</h4><p>Content spoofing / missing authorization</p><a href="https://patchstack.com/database/Wordpress/Plugin/wp-ulike/vulnerability/wordpress-wp-ulike-plugin-4-7-9-1-content-spoofing-vulnerability?_s_id=cve">Read advisory <span class="sk-visually-hidden">for CVE-2025-32259</span>↗</a></article><article><p class="about-record-id">CVE-2024-8440 <span>Medium · 6.4</span></p><h4>Essential Addons for Elementor – Popular Elementor Templates &amp; Widgets</h4><p>Stored cross-site scripting</p><a href="https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/vulnerabilities/id/c5960396-5320-4978-aa82-2e33700daa43?source=cve">Read advisory <span class="sk-visually-hidden">for CVE-2024-8440</span>↗</a></article><article><p class="about-record-id">CVE-2024-2793 <span>High · 7.2</span></p><h4>Atarim – Visual Feedback, Review &amp; AI Collaboration</h4><p>Stored cross-site scripting</p><a href="https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/vulnerabilities/id/9bd63003-d1d6-480a-8df7-878bcc89f1ee?source=cve">Read advisory <span class="sk-visually-hidden">for CVE-2024-2793</span>↗</a></article><article><p class="about-record-id">CVE-2023-37393 <span>High · 7.1</span></p><h4>Visual Website Collaboration, Feedback &amp; Project Management – Atarim</h4><p>Reflected cross-site scripting</p><a href="https://patchstack.com/database/vulnerability/atarim-visual-collaboration/wordpress-atarim-plugin-3-9-3-reflected-cross-site-scripting-xss-vulnerability?_s_id=cve">Read advisory <span class="sk-visually-hidden">for CVE-2023-37393</span>↗</a></article></div></div>
<div class="sk-accordion about-disclosures" data-no-scramble=""><details id="disclosure-index"><summary>Explore all 40 verified disclosures</summary><div class="about-index-body"><p id="disclosure-method">Scores are the assigning authority’s CVSS 3.1 assessments; dates are CVE Program publication dates. Wordfence may use different scores or publication dates. Each record links to the advisory and researcher credit. <a href="/assets/data/security-discoveries.json">Download the sourced dataset (JSON)</a>.</p><form class="sk-search-input about-filter" hidden="" role="search"><label for="disclosure-search">Search disclosures</label><div><input aria-controls="disclosure-records" id="disclosure-search" placeholder="CVE ID, product, or vulnerability" type="search"/><button class="sk-button" data-variant="secondary" type="reset">Clear</button></div><label for="disclosure-severity">Severity</label><select aria-controls="disclosure-records" id="disclosure-severity"><option value="">All severities</option><option value="High">High</option><option value="Medium">Medium</option></select><p aria-atomic="true" aria-live="polite" id="disclosure-status" role="status"></p></form><ul class="about-records" data-no-scramble="" id="disclosure-records"><li data-severity="Medium"><div class="about-record-heading"><h4><a href="https://github.com/CVEProject/cvelistV5/blob/main/cves/2025/5xxx/CVE-2025-5537.json">CVE-2025-5537</a></h4><span class="sk-badge">Medium · 6.4</span></div><p class="about-record-product">Lightbox &amp; Modal Popup WordPress Plugin – FooBox</p><p>Stored cross-site scripting</p><dl><div><dt>Published</dt><dd><time datetime="2025-07-08">2025-07-08</time></dd></div><div><dt>CVSS 3.1 source</dt><dd>Wordfence</dd></div></dl><p class="about-record-links"><a href="https://www.wordfence.com/threat-intel/vulnerabilities/id/b5fd2163-b8ef-4dd1-a12b-cd9187145134?source=cve">Advisory</a><a href="https://github.com/CVEProject/cvelistV5/blob/main/cves/2025/5xxx/CVE-2025-5537.json">Re